# Round 12 summary — close the deal on the two-level head

Decision rules pinned in `round12_analyze.py`'s docstring at 22:31 CET on 2026-11-10 (copy: `runs/round12/round12_analyze_docstring_2231.py.txt`), before any round-12 run or eval started. Seed 0 unless stated.

## Part A — LAMBADA sanity check

EleutherAI/lambada_openai test, 5,153 passages; target word 'appears earlier' in the passage (whole word, exact): n = 4,201; rest n = 952.

| model | whole-passage BPB | last word bits/byte | appears earlier | rest |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| B384 | 1.6273 | 1.7147 | 1.6956 | 1.7991 |
| H3s384_575M | 1.6074 | 1.7042 | 1.6893 | 1.7702 |
| F8k384_631M | 1.6281 | 1.3986 | 1.3063 | 1.8062 |
| F4k384_700M | 1.6441 | 1.3187 | 1.2078 | 1.8077 |

**Verdict (pinned): whole-passage BPB(F8k) − BPB(H3*) = +0.0206; F8k's last-word advantage (H3* − F8k bits/byte) on 'appears earlier' targets +0.3830 vs -0.0360 on the rest → real effect.**

Ten passages with token splits and per-token NLL: `runs/round12/partA_examples.md`. In all ten, the target is a rare name that occurs earlier in the passage. GPT-2's 50k vocabulary has it as one rare token (about 15–25 bits); the 8k vocabulary splits it into a first piece plus continuation pieces that are nearly free to copy from context. B384 ≈ H3* on this split, so the effect belongs to the vocabulary, not the head (post-hoc reading).

